   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT making appropriations for the support of government",
    "basePrintNo": "S6081",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6081-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Appropriations",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6081\n\nSPONSOR: RULES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act making appropriations for the support of government\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill provides appropriations to various State departments and agen-\ncies to permit certain payments due from April 1 to April 8, 2021, to be\nmade absent enactment of the Budget appropriation bills submitted by the\nGovernor for the State fiscal year beginning April 1, 2021.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 authorizes the Comptroller to utilize the appropriations\ncontained in this bill, which relate to the 2021-22 State fiscal year,\nabsent enactment of the 2021-22 Budget.\n\nSection 2 provides $471 million in appropriation authority for personal\nservice payments scheduled to be made to State officers and employees,\nand statewide elected officials through April 8, 2021, including\npayments for officers and employees of the Legislature and Judiciary;\nstatewide elected officials and elected members of the Legislature, and\npayments for services performed by mentally ill or developmentally disa-\nbled persons who are employed in State operated special employment, work\nfor pay or sheltered workshop programs.\n\nSection 3 provides $65 million in appropriation authority for state\nagencies to pay fringe benefit and indirect cost assessments incurred\nagainst personal service liabilities prior to April 1, 2021, and during\nthe period April 1 through April 8, 2021.\n\nSection 4 provides $22 million in appropriation authority for nonper-\nsonal service payments by various State agencies including the Legisla-\nture. It is the intent of this section to provide sufficient authori-\nzation for agencies to enter into contracts, the terms of which may\ncontinue beyond the life of this appropriation and for which payments\nfor liabilities incurred beyond April 8, 2021 would be made subject to\nadditional future appropriations.\n\nSection 5 provides $37 million in appropriation authority to cover\nexisting capital projects contract costs approved prior to April 1, 2021\nfor all agencies.\n\nSection 6 provides $3 million in appropriation authority to cover exist-\ning capital projects contract costs approved after April 1, 2021 for all\nagencies.\n\nSection 7 provides:\n\n* $35 million in appropriation authority for nonpersonal service liabil-\nities incurred by the Judiciary.\n\n* $100,000 in appropriation authority for aid to localities liabilities\nincurred by the Judiciary.\n\n\n* $47 million in aid to localities appropriation authority to be subal-\nlocated to the Office of Indigent Legal Services by the Judiciary.\n\n* $5 million in appropriation authority for various employee fringe\nbenefit programs within the Judiciary.\n\nSection 8 provides a total of $600.4 million in appropriation authority\nfor payment of State employee and retiree fringe benefits and other\nfixed costs mandated by statute or collective bargaining agreement\nduring the period April 1 to April 8, 2021, including: New York State\nHealth Insurance Plan coverage, the State's contributions towards its\nretirement obligation within the voluntary defined contribution plan,\nthe Social Security payroll tax, workers' compensation coverage, dental\nand vision coverage (including coverage provided through union-specific\nEmployee Benefit Funds), taxes on State-owned lands, the Metropolitan\nCommuter Transportation Mobility Tax, judgements against the State\npursuant to the Court of Claims Act, legal expenses pursuant to the\nPublic Officers' Law, Management and Confidential employees income\nprotection plan, unemployment insurance, and automobile accident claims.\nThe amounts paid during this period will ensure the continuation of\nhealth insurance and other critical benefits for State employees / reti-\nrees.\n\nSection 9 provides $46 million in appropriation authority for Safety Net\nAssistance and $108 million in Family Assistance. These benefits\npayments are set in statute, though the timing of the actual payment is\nflexible. Appropriation authority is necessary due to restrictive\nlanguage for reimbursement of program costs. This section also provides\n$58 million in appropriation authority for the state Supplemental Secu-\nrity Income (SSI) program, which makes monthly cash payments to aged,\nblind, and disabled low-income New Yorkers, and by statute must be\nreceived by the first of the month.\n\nSection 10 provides $3.2 million in appropriation authority to the\nDepartment of Health for the Center for Community Health Program. The\nfunds are in support of the American Indian Health Care Program to\nprovide payments to support pharmacy providers. In addition, this\nsection provides $16 million in appropriation authority for the Special\nSupplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants, and Children Program.\nThe funds are in support of daily draws that reimburse vendors for\nservices provided.\n\nSection 11 provides $105,000 in appropriation authority for the Justice\nCenter for the Protection of People with Special Needs to support the\nSurrogate Decision-Making Committee.\n\nSection 12 provides $5 billion in appropriation which is necessary to\nensure unemployment insurance benefits are payable to claimants. As of\nMarch 4, 2021 there is $6.5 billion left in FY 2021 authority for this\nprogram and recent monthly disbursements have averaged around $5.4\nbillion. Much of the unemployment insurance program is currently\nsupported with additional Federal dollars.\n\nSection 13 provides $98 million in appropriation authority to the Office\nfor People with Developmental Disabilities to support not-for-profit\nproviders of essential programs and services.\n\n\nSection 14 provides $407,000 to the Division of Veterans' Services to\nmake payments through the Blind Veteran Annuity Assistance Program to\nits annuitants on a timely basis.\n\nSection 15 prohibits expenditures from all appropriations until certif-\nicates of approval have been issued by the Director of the Budget and\nfiled with certain State officers.\n\nSection 16 requires the Comptroller to transfer any expenditures made\nagainst these appropriations to the 2021-22 Budget appropriations after\nthey have become law.\n\nSection 17, the severability clause, provides that if any part of this\nAct be adjudged by any court of competent jurisdiction to be invalid,\nsuch judgment would not invalidate the remainder of the Act.\n\nSection 18 provides that the bill takes effect immediately and is deemed\nto be in full force and effect on April 1, 2021, and, further, that the\nappropriations made in the bill will be deemed repealed upon the trans-\nfer of expenditures by the Comptroller pursuant to section 16 of the\nbill.\n\n \nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nThis bill will allow the State to make certain payments and incur\ncertain liabilities during the period April 1 through April 8, 2021 on a\ntimely basis, in the absence of an enacted budget for State fiscal year\n2021-22.\n\n \nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nExpenditures and disbursements made against these appropriations shall,\nupon final action by the Legislature on the appropriation bills submit-\nted by the Governor for the support of government for the State fiscal\nyear beginning April 1, 2021, be transferred by the Comptroller as\nexpenditures and disbursements to such appropriations for State depart-\nments and agencies and the Legislature and Judiciary.  Accordingly, this\nbill will have no additional impact on the State's 2021-22 Financial\nPlan.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
